-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/b6ff7074-6f33-407b-a415-df935b330a93
-- title:
--   Proof of Theorem 4.4, p. 25 — exponentials of multi-time sums $\sum_k(f_k)_{\tau_k}$, $\tau_k>0$, span a dense subspace of $\mathcal E_+$ (corrected: products of exponentials at different times)
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $M$ is a quantizable static spacetime (Definition 4.1): a complete, connected, smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension $d$ with a global static Killing flow $\phi_s$ (each $\phi_s$ an isometry), a smooth time function $t$ with $t(\phi_s p)=t(p)+s$, the time-zero slice $\Sigma=\{t=0\}$ orthogonal to the Killing field $\xi$, the regions $\Omega_\pm=\{\pm t>0\}$, the reflection $\theta$ and the lapse $F=\langle\xi,\xi\rangle$; $\mathrm{dvol}$ is its Riemannian volume.
--
--   Let $m>0$ and let $\Phi$ be the Euclidean free field of mass $m$. The closed span of the vectors
--   $$\exp\Big(i\Phi\Big(\sum_{k=1}^n (f_k)_{\tau_k}\Big)\Big),\qquad n\ge0,\ f_k\in C_c^\infty(\Sigma,\mathbb R),\ \tau_k>0,$$
--   is exactly $\mathcal E_+$; equivalently, the algebra generated by $\bigcup_{t>0}\mathcal E_t$ is dense in $\mathcal E_+$ (and contained in it).
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: the span of single-time exponentials, ⋃_{t>0} ℰ_t, is dense in ℰ₊ → the span of exponentials of finite sums Σ_k (f_k)_{τ_k} with τ_k > 0 is dense in ℰ₊ (and lies in it); reason: single-time spans are not dense: on flat ℝ^d (d ≥ 2, any m > 0) take 0 < t₁ < t₂, u = f_{t₁} + g_{t₂}; the second-chaos projection maps the closed span of single-time exponentials into the closed span of {h_t ⊗ h_t}, which is orthogonal to v ⊗ w for v = (−Δ+m²)φ₁, w = (−Δ+m²)φ₂ with φ₁, φ₂ supported in disjoint time slabs around t₁, t₂, while ⟨u ⊗ u, v ⊗ w⟩ ≠ 0; the Riemann-sum argument of the proof produces products of exponentials at different times
--   - reading: the class of f is not specified (the proof writes g ∈ 𝒯_Σ, never defined) → real f ∈ C_c^∞(Σ), encoded by smooth ξ-invariant lifts f : M → ℝ with Σ ∩ tsupport f compact; reason: smallest natural class; it makes density hardest and isometry/membership extend to closures (Reading 2)
--   - encoding: H₋₁(M) as the completion of C^∞(M) in ⟨f, Cf⟩, ⟨f, Cg⟩ with C = (−Δ+m²)⁻¹, and dvol → ‖ℓ‖₋₁ = sup of |ℓ(u)| over u ∈ C_c^∞(M) with ∫(|∇u|²+m²u²) dvol ≤ 1, ⟨·, C·⟩ by polarization, dvol characterized by its chart densities √det g; reason: on a complete M, −Δ is essentially self-adjoint on C_c^∞ and this dual norm is ⟨f, Cf⟩^{1/2}; same objects (Reading 4)
--   - reading: E_Ω = span{e^{iΦ(f)} : f ∈ H₋₁(M), supp f ⊂ Ω} → closed span over real f ∈ C_c^∞(M) with tsupport f ⊆ Ω; reason: the proof of Theorem 4.4 describes ℰ₊ by smooth compactly supported positive-time test functions; the closures coincide (Reading 5)
--   - generalization: the Gaussian measure µ on H₋∞ with covariance C (Minlos), ℰ = L²(H₋∞, µ) → any isonormal Gaussian field Φ on H₋₁(M) over any probability space (Ω, P), ℰ = L²(Ω, P; ℂ); reason: Minlos’ measure is one such field and every statement depends only on the joint law (Reading 6)
-- source:
--   Jaffe, Ritter, Quantum Field Theory on Curved Backgrounds. I. The Euclidean Functional Integral, arXiv:hep-th/0609003v1, proof of Theorem 4.4, p. 25 ("Thus ⋃_{t>0} ℰ_t is dense in ℰ₊")

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_IsRiemannianVolume
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_IsGaussianField
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_positiveTimeSubspace
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_sharpTime
import Definitions.Def_JRCurvedQFT_SharpTime_IsSliceTestFunction

open scoped Manifold ContDiff ENNReal Topology
open Bundle MeasureTheory

namespace JRCurvedQFT.SharpTime

/-- **Proof of Theorem 4.4, p. 25 (corrected).** Exponentials of finite sums of positive-time
sharp-time distributions span a dense subspace of `ℰ₊`.

Jaffe, Ritter, *Quantum Field Theory on Curved Backgrounds. I. The Euclidean Functional Integral*, arXiv:hep-th/0609003v1, proof of Theorem 4.4, p. 25: "Eqn. (4.15) represents Φ(f) as a limit of linear combinations
of elements Φ(f_{t_i}) ∈ ℰ_{t_i}. A similar argument applies to polynomials A(Φ) of classical fields,
and to L² limits of such polynomials. Thus ⋃_{t>0} ℰ_t is dense in ℰ₊."

Setting (Definition 4.1): `M` is a complete, connected, smooth Riemannian manifold of dimension
`d` (charts in `ℝ^d`, smooth metric, distance = Riemannian distance) with a static structure `S`
(flow `φ_s` of the static Killing field, time function `t`, slice `Σ = {t = 0}`), and `μ` is its
Riemannian volume. `m > 0` and `Φ` is the free field. Statement: the closed span of the vectors
`exp(iΦ(Σ_{k<n} (f_k)_{τ_k}))`, over `n ∈ ℕ`, real test functions `f_k` on `Σ` and times `τ_k > 0`,
equals `ℰ₊` (in particular each such vector lies in `ℰ₊`).

**Formalization Note** Correction: the span of single-time exponentials `⋃_{t>0} ℰ_t` is not dense
(a second-chaos functional separates `e^{iΦ(f_{t₁} + g_{t₂})}` from it on flat `ℝ^d`, `d ≥ 2`); the
Riemann-sum argument produces products of exponentials at different times, i.e. exponentials of
multi-time sums. `n = 0` gives the constant `1 ∈ ℰ₊`. -/
theorem multiTime_dense {d : ℕ} {M : Type} [EMetricSpace M]
    [ChartedSpace (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) M] [IsManifold 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) ∞ M]
    [RiemannianBundle (fun (x : M) ↦ TangentSpace 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) x)]
    [IsContMDiffRiemannianBundle 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) ∞ (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))
      (fun (x : M) ↦ TangentSpace 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) x)]
    [IsRiemannianManifold 𝓘(ℝ, EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)) M] [CompleteSpace M] [ConnectedSpace M]
    [MeasurableSpace M] [BorelSpace M]
    (S : StaticStructure (d := d) M) (μ : Measure M) (hμ : IsRiemannianVolume (d := d) μ)
    (m : ℝ) (hm : 0 < m) {Ω : Type} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω) [IsProbabilityMeasure P]
    (Φ : ((M → ℝ) → ℝ) → Ω → ℝ) (hΦ : IsGaussianField (d := d) μ m P Φ) :
    (Submodule.span ℂ {A | ∃ (n : ℕ) (f : Fin n → M → ℝ) (τ : Fin n → ℝ),
      (∀ k, IsSliceTestFunction S (f k) ∧ 0 < τ k) ∧
      A = expVec P Φ (∑ k, sharpTime S μ (f k) (τ k))}).topologicalClosure
      = positiveTimeSubspace S μ P Φ := by sorry

end JRCurvedQFT.SharpTime
